The Security Council,

Recalling the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations, including the sovereign equality of all Member States, territorial integrity, political independence, the non-use of force or threat of force, and the peaceful settlement of disputes,

Reaffirming its primary responsibility under the Charter for the maintenance of international peace and security, and the importance of multilateralism and diplomacy in addressing international disputes,

Having considered the letter dated 1 July 2025 from the Permanent Representative of Pakistan to the United Nations addressed to the Secretary-General (S/2025/443),

Deeply concerned by the military escalation in South Asia in May 2025, including reports of cross-border airstrikes, exchanges of fire and other military actions, which have caused loss of civilian life, heightened tensions and threatened regional and international peace and security,

Deploring the loss of life in the terrorist attack of 22 April 2025 in Pahalgam, Jammu and Kashmir, and stressing the need for a thorough, impartial and transparent investigation to bring the perpetrators, organizers, financiers and sponsors of this and other terrorist acts to justice,

Condemning all acts of terrorism in all its forms and manifestations, irrespective of their motivation, and reaffirming that terrorism constitutes one of the most serious threats to international peace and security,

Welcoming the ceasefire understanding of 10 May 2025 and the subsequent restoration of relative calm, while expressing concern at reports of violations and continued risks of escalation,

Stressing that there can be no military solution to the outstanding disputes between India and Pakistan and that dialogue, diplomacy and peaceful means, including those provided for in the Charter and relevant bilateral agreements, are essential for durable peace and stability,

Reaffirming the importance of respecting existing bilateral agreements and arrangements, including those relating to shared water resources, in accordance with international law and the principles of good-neighbourliness,

Recognizing the need for unhindered humanitarian access and protection of civilians, in accordance with international humanitarian law,

1. Demands the immediate and full cessation of hostilities and respect for the ceasefire along the Line of Control and the international border, and calls upon all parties to exercise maximum restraint, de-escalate tensions and avoid any further military action;

2. Condemns all acts of terrorism and calls upon all States to cooperate fully in preventing and suppressing terrorist acts and in bringing to justice the perpetrators, organizers, financiers and sponsors of the terrorist attack of 22 April 2025 in Pahalgam, Jammu and Kashmir, and other terrorist acts, in accordance with international law and relevant Security Council resolutions;

3. Urges the Governments of India and Pakistan to engage, in good faith and without preconditions, in direct dialogue and diplomacy, including through established bilateral mechanisms and with the good offices of the Secretary-General, with a view to peacefully resolving all outstanding disputes and ensuring durable peace and stability in South Asia;

4. Calls upon all parties to respect the sovereignty, territorial integrity and political independence of States, and to refrain from any unilateral action that could alter the situation on the ground or undermine the ceasefire and the prospects for peaceful settlement;

5. Urges all parties to respect existing bilateral agreements and arrangements, including those relating to shared water resources, and to refrain from any action that could endanger water security, livelihoods and the welfare of civilian populations;

6. Calls for the protection of civilians and for unhindered humanitarian access to affected populations, and urges all parties to ensure the safety and security of civilians and civilian infrastructure;

7. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to use his good offices to facilitate de-escalation, dialogue and the peaceful settlement of disputes between the parties, and to report to the Security Council on the implementation of this resolution within 60 days;

8. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

The Security Council,

