Stressing that there can be no military solution to the outstanding disputes between India and Pakistan and that dialogue, diplomacy and peaceful means, including those provided for in the Charter and relevant bilateral agreements, are essential for durable peace and stability,

Reaffirming the importance of respecting existing bilateral agreements and arrangements to which States are parties, in accordance with international law and the principles of good-neighbourliness,

Recognizing the need for unhindered humanitarian access and protection of civilians, in accordance with international humanitarian law,

Recognizing the importance of the full, equal and meaningful participation of women in conflict prevention, resolution and peacebuilding, and the particular impact of armed conflict on children,

1. Demands the immediate and full cessation of hostilities and respect for the ceasefire along the Line of Control and the international border, and calls upon all parties to exercise maximum restraint, de-escalate tensions and avoid any further military action;

2. Condemns all acts of terrorism and calls upon all States to cooperate fully in preventing and suppressing terrorist acts and in bringing to justice the perpetrators, organizers, financiers and sponsors of the terrorist attack of 22 April 2025 in Pahalgam, Jammu and Kashmir, and other terrorist acts, in accordance with international law and relevant Security Council resolutions, including by denying safe haven to terrorists and terrorist organizations and preventing the use of their territories for the planning, financing or perpetration of terrorist acts, and stresses that counterterrorism cooperation must be conducted without politicization and double standards and with full respect for the sovereignty and territorial integrity of all States;

3. Urges the Governments of India and Pakistan to engage, in good faith and without preconditions, on the basis of mutual respect and in accordance with the Charter and the need to combat terrorism, in direct dialogue and diplomacy and other peaceful means available under the Charter, including, as appropriate, the peaceful means set out in Article 33 of the Charter, such as mediation, conciliation, arbitration and judicial settlement, including through established bilateral mechanisms and, if the parties so agree, with the good offices of the Secretary-General, with a view to peacefully resolving all outstanding disputes, including the Jammu and Kashmir dispute, in accordance with relevant Security Council resolutions and the Charter, and ensuring durable peace and stability in South Asia;

4. Calls upon all parties to respect the sovereignty, territorial integrity and political independence of States and the principle of non-interference in the internal affairs of States, in accordance with the Charter and international law, and to refrain from any unilateral action that could alter the situation on the ground or undermine the ceasefire and the prospects for peaceful settlement;

5. Urges all parties to respect existing bilateral agreements and arrangements to which they are parties, in accordance with international law, without prejudice to their respective legal positions and obligations, and to refrain from any action that could endanger the welfare of civilian populations;

6. Calls upon all parties to comply strictly with their obligations under international humanitarian law and international human rights law, to protect civilians and civilian infrastructure, including medical and educational facilities, and humanitarian personnel, and to ensure safe, rapid, timely and unhindered humanitarian access to affected populations, in accordance with international humanitarian law and the humanitarian principles, and urges all parties to ensure the safety and security of civilians, humanitarian personnel and civilian infrastructure;

7. Calls upon all parties to ensure independent, impartial and transparent investigations into all alleged violations of international humanitarian law and human rights abuses, to ensure accountability, and to protect vulnerable groups, including women and children, and stresses the importance of the full, equal, meaningful and safe participation of women in all efforts to prevent and resolve conflict and in peace processes, in accordance with the Women, Peace and Security agenda;

8. Invites the Secretary-General, in close consultation with and with the consent of the parties, to continue to use his good offices, in coordination with relevant regional organizations, including the African Union and the Organization of Islamic Cooperation, as appropriate, to facilitate de-escalation, dialogue and the peaceful settlement of disputes between the parties, and requests him to report to the Security Council within 60 days on the implementation of this resolution, including on ceasefire compliance, humanitarian access, and progress in direct dialogue, with attention to the participation of women and the protection of children;

9. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

